#%PAM-1.0

# NOTE: pam_faillock is intentionally NOT used here.
#
# faillock keeps a SINGLE system-wide failure tally shared across every PAM
# service (sudo, login, this lock screen). In practice that meant unattended
# `sudo -A` askpass prompts — e.g. a Claude agent's sudo commands rewritten by
# claude-sudo-askpass-hook.sh and left unanswered while away — each counted as a
# failed auth and, combined with the odd mistype, tripped deny=3 and locked the
# user out of their OWN screen (the lock was the victim, not the attacker).
#
# Decoupling the lock from that shared tally fixes it: lock-screen password
# attempts no longer increment OR read faillock, so sudo spam can't lock the
# screen and a few mistypes are free. The lock keeps its own retry/backoff UI
# (modules/lock/Pam.qml). sudo and login retain faillock via /etc/pam.d.
# Rationale: docs/lock-crash-observability.md + project memory (2026-06-09).
auth    required    pam_unix.so     nullok
